{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C1DostQ2rzJ3S7ZKexKE2a","type":"tool_result","content":"tree-shaking (FR-5, FR-19), exact stripping with loud failure\n(FR-20 through FR-23), the fixed-string marker check with a positive\ncontrol asserted first per ADR-004 (FR-37), and an application-level\ngate over the example's built output (FR-38).\n\n### The narrowed serialization payload\n\nAlongside the delivery change, the state payload narrows to exactly\nwhat HTML cannot express. Three measurements, taken 2026-08-18\nagainst this toolchain and inherited unchanged from the superseded\nPRD, are the basis:\n\n- **Double data costs roughly its full standalone size.** Fifty\n  rendered products plus the same data as a JSON payload: HTML alone\n  509 bytes brotli, payload alone 496, both together 967. The\n  compressor recovers 7.7 percent. A control appending a\n  byte-identical copy of the HTML costs 8 bytes, showing compression\n  dedups repeated text, not the same values wrapped in different\n  syntax. At n=1000 the payload costs 1.3 percent more than\n  standalone, because it dilutes the HTML's own compression context.\n- **A library deserializer is unaffordable at this scale.** Client-side\n  import cost, brotli: devalue `parse` 1959 bytes, turbo-stream\n  `decode` 2477, seroval `fromJSON` 3425, flatted `parse` 377,\n  `JSON.parse` baseline 32. The core is 3519, so devalue alone adds\n  56 percent. A hand-written reviver under 250 bytes replaces all of\n  them.\n- **HTML is already lossless for this component model.** React RFC\n  0188 (line 518) justifies its custom protocol by prop transfer and\n  tree reconciliation, so client state and DOM focus survive. Here the\n  tag name is the component identity and the attributes are the props,\n  so `<my-counter start=\"5\">` already is the lossless form. On the\n  same 20-post tree the RSC payload measured 9505 raw bytes against\n  7394 of HTML -- 129 percent of the markup it duplicates.\n\nWhat remains genuinely unrepresentable is narrow: property-only props\ndeclared with `p.prop()`, which `src/with-render.ts` skips from HTML\nentirely, and state values no attribute carries. Only those serialize,\ninto a per-element `<script type=\"application/json\">`, revived on\nconnect. Attribute-backed props stay attributes.\n\n## Consequences\n\nEasier:\n\n- A component is one file. The author writes markup next to behavior\n  and never maintains a second module or an import convention.\n/**\n * The client half of the state payload convention. The server half --\n * the tags, the `$` escaping, and the script element they travel in --\n * lives in `src/payload.ts`, which is server-only and must never be\n * imported from here: this module is client-reachable by design, and\n * `npm run test:bundle` guards that edge. Restating the convention here\n * is deliberate; it is the seam between two bundles, so keep the two\n * files in sync.\n *\n * The whole module is written to a byte budget: deserialization ships\n * to the browser. Deserialization proper -- wireName, TAGS and\n * reviveValue, everything but the final merge into the bag --\n * measures 244 B brotli built minified, six bytes under the budget\n * ADR-005 sets. Measured by size-limit's own pipeline (esbuild\n * re-bundle, then brotli quality 11, less its 16-byte empty project\n * constant); plain q11 brotli of the minified file reads 280, so\n * quote the method with any figure. That is the whole point of hand-\n * writing it: it replaces general serializers an order of magnitude\n * larger (ADR-005 measured devalue at 1959 B, turbo-stream at 2477 B,\n * seroval at 3425 B, flatted at 377 B).\n *\n * Nothing here probes a parsed object by an arbitrary name. Every\n * name read off the wire arrives through `Object.keys`, which\n * enumerates own enumerable properties only -- an inherited property\n * can neither impersonate data nor ride along -- and the one name that\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/nick/code/microtags.millitags-lessons-11-adr-008-note-with-measurements","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0efd265-b92a-42b7-bd22-d60c16f119d9","uuid":"8927cbf6-243b-42e2-a7aa-21585689e74d","timestamp":"2026-10-15T04:09:25.069Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"tree-shaking (FR-5, FR-19), exact stripping with loud failure\n(FR-20 through FR-23), the fixed-string marker check with a positive\ncontrol asserted first per ADR-004 (FR-37), and an application-level\ngate over the example's built output (FR-38).\n\n### The narrowed serialization payload\n\nAlongside the delivery change, the state payload narrows to exactly\nwhat HTML cannot express. Three measurements, taken 2026-08-18\nagainst this toolchain and inherited unchanged from the superseded\nPRD, are the basis:\n\n- **Double data costs roughly its full standalone size.** Fifty\n  rendered products plus the same data as a JSON payload: HTML alone\n  509 bytes brotli, payload alone 496, both together 967. The\n  compressor recovers 7.7 percent. A control appending a\n  byte-identical copy of the HTML costs 8 bytes, showing compression\n  dedups repeated text, not the same values wrapped in different\n  syntax. At n=1000 the payload costs 1.3 percent more than\n  standalone, because it dilutes the HTML's own compression context.\n- **A library deserializer is unaffordable at this scale.** Client-side\n  import cost, brotli: devalue `parse` 1959 bytes, turbo-stream\n  `decode` 2477, seroval `fromJSON` 3425, flatted `parse` 377,\n  `JSON.parse` baseline 32. The core is 3519, so devalue alone adds\n  56 percent. A hand-written reviver under 250 bytes replaces all of\n  them.\n- **HTML is already lossless for this component model.** React RFC\n  0188 (line 518) justifies its custom protocol by prop transfer and\n  tree reconciliation, so client state and DOM focus survive. Here the\n  tag name is the component identity and the attributes are the props,\n  so `<my-counter start=\"5\">` already is 