{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg3NE8Qti8mAGYY4QQfc4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01W6v14pJUJ9av587M783Ky5","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat >> docs/adr/ADR-008-serialized-server-state.md <<'EOF'\n\n## Note (2026-10-14): measurements behind the duplication trade-off\n\nThis note does not change the decision. It records numbers that make\nthe \"state is duplicated\" trade-off a known, bounded cost.\n\nThe measurements are from the sibling repo millitags, taken\n2026-08-18 with its toolchain and recorded in its ADR-005 (\"The\nnarrowed serialization payload\"). They were not re-run here.\n\n- Duplicated data costs about its full standalone size. Fifty rendered\n  products plus the same data as a JSON payload: HTML alone 509 bytes\n  brotli, payload alone 496, both together 967. The compressor\n  recovers 7.7 percent. A control appending a byte-identical copy of\n  the HTML costs 8 bytes, so compression dedups repeated text, not the\n  same values wrapped in different syntax.\n- A library deserializer is expensive at this scale. Client-side\n  import cost, brotli: devalue `parse` 1959 bytes, turbo-stream\n  `decode` 2477, seroval `fromJSON` 3425, flatted `parse` 377,\n  `JSON.parse` baseline 32. Millitags' core was 3519 bytes, so each of\n  the first three is more than half of a core bundle (devalue alone\n  adds 56 percent), and all four are larger than its hand-written\n  reviver, which measured under 250 bytes.\n\nConclusion: the payload stays narrow and `JSON.parse` stays the\nreviver. The duplication costs roughly the payload's own compressed\nsize, so keeping `AppState` to the values the page needs keeps that\ncost small; and no general deserializer is worth its bytes when\n`AppState` holds JSON values only. This is what the Decision above\nalready says.\nEOF\ngrep -n \"—\\|→\" docs/adr/ADR-008-serialized-server-state.md; git diff --stat"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1556,"cache_read_input_tokens":52415,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1556},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0efd265-b92a-42b7-bd22-d60c16f119d9","uuid":"43dd6271-f089-4e69-a3ba-d25097625ba6","timestamp":"2026-10-15T04:09:33.505Z","request_id":"req_011Cg3NE89mbL6buydCeypHQ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01W6v14pJUJ9av587M783Ky5":{"command":"cat >> docs/adr/ADR-008-serialized-server-state.md <<'EOF'\n\n## Note (2026-10-14): measurements behind the duplication trade-off\n\nThis note does not change the decision. It records numbers that make\nthe \"state is duplicated\" trade-off a known, bounded cost.\n\nThe measurements are from the sibling repo millitags, taken\n2026-08-18 with its toolchain and recorded in its ADR-005 (\"The\nnarrowed serialization payload\"). They were not re-run here.\n\n- Duplicated data costs about its full standalone size. Fifty rendered\n  products plus the same data as a JSON payload: HTML alone 509 bytes\n  brotli, payload alone 496, both together 967. The compressor\n  recovers 7.7 percent. A control appending a byte-identical copy of\n  the HTML costs 8 bytes, so compression dedups repeated text, not the\n  same values wrapped in different syntax.\n- A library deserializer is expensive at this scale. Client-side\n  import cost, brotli: devalue `parse` 1959 bytes, turbo-stream\n  `decode` 2477, seroval `fromJSON` 3425, flatted `parse` 377,\n  `JSON.parse` baseline 32. Millitags' core was 3519 bytes, so each of\n  the first three is more than half of a core bundle (devalue alone\n  adds 56 percent), and all four are larger than its hand-written\n  reviver, which measured under 250 bytes.\n\nConclusion: the payload stays narrow and `JSON.parse` stays the\nreviver. The duplication costs roughly the payload's own compressed\nsize, so keeping `AppState` to the values the page needs keeps that\ncost small; and no general deserializer is worth its bytes when\n`AppState` holds JSON values only. This is what the Decision above\nalready says.\nEOF\ngrep -n \"—\\|→\" docs/adr/ADR-008-serialized-server-state.md; git diff --stat"}}}
